PRE-TABLE BLOCK -- #524 armbdt, TABLE 7 (h): THE TEN-CELL FLOOR
==============================================================
SEPARATE FILE, not an append.  WRITTEN BEFORE TABLE 1 RUNS, so this file's own
mtime proves the order.

*** THIS IS THE FIRST TABLE IN THIS ROUND WHOSE ROW SET IS DERIVED FROM THE
HELPERS' INPUTS RATHER THAN FROM THE MUTANT LIST, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE
WHOLE REASON THE CHAIN RAN TO FOUR PASSES. ***

Passes A/B/C/D each repaired at the residue of the mutant they had just found --
2 for `~1', bit-2-set for `~7' -- so each pass necessarily discovered the next
strength.  Pass C invoked the class-closure amendment and then executed it FROM
THE MUTANT LIST; the probe's own comment admits it ("a base at residue 2 ...
which is where ~1 and ~3 disagree").  The amendment says enumerate from the
FUNCTION'S OWN INPUTS.  This block is that enumeration.

--------------------------------------------------------------- THE FLOOR

Adjudicated 2026-09-11 (regress/artifacts/armbdt/passD/).  The joint-cell rule's
"body" is the GUARDED body -- the cold `else' branch -- NOT the enclosing
function.  The guarded body of the pop edit (cpu_arm_instr.c:1652-1730) tests
exactly two things: the memory_rw return, and cpu->byte_order.  So p/u/s/w are
SELECTORS tested outside it, and the join rule asks whether each flows into an
lvalue the guarded body reads or writes:

    p, u  -> `addr'                                   FLOOR, both sites
    s     -> pop: nothing (gates return_flag and the
                  second loop's bank select, AFTER
                  the body)                           clause (e) on pop
             push: `value' (:1848-1865), which the
                  body packs into `data' (:1894-1904) FLOOR on push
    w     -> nothing at either site (`*np = addr' is
             after the loop, :1777 / :1925)           clause (e), both sites
    i == ARM_PC -> push: `value = cpu->pc + 12'       FLOOR on push

*** AND p/u ARE ONE SELECTOR WITH FOUR VALUES, NOT TWO BITS. ***  The code tests
them NESTED (`if (p_bit) { if (u_bit)'), the architecture names the pair as one
field, and generate_arm_multi.c:120 switches on `p*2+u'.  So a mutant
conditioned on any SUBSET of modes is killed by any member cell's row, and
`(p && u)' is not a joint -- it is the cell IB.

    FLOOR = {cold first access} x {pop, push} x {IA, IB, DA, DB}
          + {push} x {s = 1}
          + {push} x {pc in list}                         = TEN CELLS

------------------------------------------- THE GEOMETRY: RESIDUE 7 (mod 8)

One cold DATA row per cell, at an access address = 7 (mod 8).  0x_0007 has bits
0, 1 AND 2 set, so ONE access discriminates `~1', `~2', `~3' AND `~7'.  No
future strength needs a new residue, which is the property every previous repair
lacked.

*** THIS ALSO CLOSES A LIVE GAP THE PREVIOUS TABLE SHIPPED: all three pass-C
closure rows accessed 0x20006, bit 0 CLEAR, so `addr & (p_bit ? 0xfffffffd :
0xfffffffc)' escaped BY ARITHMETIC.  The round's residue-3 disclosure ("~2
already dies at +1") was true of the UNCONDITIONAL form and went false the
moment pass C admitted mode-conditioning, because every +1 row is IA. ***

--------------------------------------------------- CELL -> ROW -> MUTANT

cell              row(s)                      access      kills
----------------  --------------------------  ----------  ----------------------
pop  IA           ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA           0x20001     M_MASK1 (measured 29/39)
                  ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2        0x20002
                  ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_BIT2      0x20005     M_MASK15 (30/39)
pop  IB           ARM_LDM_IB_P2               0x20007     M_POPP_*, M_NPMASK
pop  DA           ARM_LDM_DA_P2               0x20007     M_POPU_WEAK
pop  DB           ARM_LDM_DB_P2               0x20007     M_POPP_*, M_POPW_WEAK
push IA           ARM_STM_COLD_DATA           0x21001
                  ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2        0x21002
                  ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2      0x21005
push IB           ARM_STM_IB_R7      *NEW*    0x21007     M_PUSH_IB_WEAK
push DA           ARM_STM_DA_R7      *NEW*    0x21007     M_PUSH_DA_WEAK
push DB           THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA        0x23001     } bit 2 NEVER set here,
                  THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2     0x23002     } which is exactly E4
push s=1          ARM_STM_S_R7       *NEW*    0x21007     M_PUSHS_NONE  <- E1, L2
push pc-in-list   ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7   *NEW*    0x21007     M_PUSHPC_NONE  <- closes E4 too

OVER-COVERAGE, kept deliberately and NOT floor: ARM_LDM_S_P2, now
`LDMIA r0!,{r1}^' at 0x20007.  s is clause (e) on the POP side, but the row is
free and at residue 7 it also closes the (s && w) joint (E2) and the
s-conditioned over-alignment (E3).

----------------------------------------------- THE TENTH CELL: NOW WRITTEN

    STMDB r0!,{r1,pc}   = 0xe9208002   base 0x2100b     -> ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7

*** ONE ROW CLOSES TWO CELLS. ***  In DB the loop runs `for (i = (u_bit ? 0 : 15); ...)`,
so with u_bit==0 it starts at r15: PC IS ACCESSED FIRST and its access is the COLD
one, at 0x21007 -- bit 2 SET.  So this single row closes {push x pc} AND gives
{push x DB} the bit-2-set residue it never had, which is E4.

EVERY NUMBER IN IT WAS DERIVED FROM THE SOURCE BY A SEAT, NOT BY ME, AND CROSS-CHECKED
HERE.  The block was held OPEN in the first version of this file precisely because its
expectation is `cpu->pc + 12` (arm_push:1868) rather than a value I could read off a
seed -- and two rows earlier today carried expectations I derived myself, never
measured, and then mis-diagnosed twice.

    decode    0xe9208002 = STMDB r0!,{r1,pc}   cond=e P=1 U=0 S=0 W=1 L=0 Rn=0
    accesses  0x21007 (pc, FIRST, cold) then 0x21003 (r1)
    land at   0x21004 (pc) and 0x21000 (r1), after & ~3
    expect    w0 = 0x11223344   w1 = CODE + 20 = 0x00008014   r0 = 0x00021003

REACHABILITY, stated as the seat stated it rather than upgraded: there is NO PC-list
rejection in arm_push() (the S-bit path banks only through r14; the PC case takes
pc+12 unconditionally; the decoder's PC rejection at :4840 is about PC as the BASE
register, which Rn=0 is not).  **But the two files it read do NOT settle unconditional
cold-path reachability** -- PC reaches the edited call only when host_store[0x21] is
NULL, and the decoder may substitute an optimised handler.  **That is what the BASE leg
of this table measures: if ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7 is green on BASE, the path was taken.**

The model's +12 is asserted as WHAT THE MODEL DOES.  The 8-vs-12 question is a separate
TODO and this row deliberately does not arbitrate it.

--------------------------------------------------------- CLAUSE (e) DISCLOSED

Named rather than left blank, so an escape here is "outside the contract, as
stated" rather than a breach:

  * ALL w-conditioned forms, both sites (w reaches nothing the body reads).
  * ALL s-conditioned forms on POP (S_P2 is over-coverage, not floor).
  * `iw & 0x8000' on pop.
  * SECOND AND LATER REGISTERS of one instruction -- the first cold access WARMS
    the page, so every later access takes the fast path.  See [[multiaccesswarms]].
    A page-crossing second access would be the only cold form; NOT owed, because
    iteration is not an input of the mask.
  * BYTE ORDER -- a body-tested partition, and N/A-WITH-REASON rather than blank:
    there is no BE ARM rig; `-E testarm' is LE.  Note :4810-4811 disables the
    optimised path entirely for a BE guest, so on BE every block transfer goes
    through these helpers.
  * MASK CONSTANTS agreeing with 0xfffffffc on bits 0-2 and differing above.
  * JOINTS of any floor axis with any of the above.

------------------------------------------------------------- TABLE LEGS

Regression (must all still die): the six from killtable_20260911g_closure.
New, must die:  M_PUSHS_NONE, M_POPP_MASK2, M_PUSH_IB_WEAK, M_PUSH_DA_WEAK,
                M_PUSHPC_NONE, M_PUSHP_OVER7.
Disclosed controls, EXPECTED TO ESCAPE (and the contract says so): the
(s && w) and (p && u && w) forms on pop.

A leg that escapes and is NOT in the disclosed list is a floor breach.  A zero
against this ten-cell denominator closes the chain; a bare zero does not.
